A reachable score has both values non-negative, and play stops at the first winning point. Suppose A has won, meaning p ≥ 11 and p − q ≥ 2. Then the score just before A's last point, (p−1, q), must not have been a win already. So it cannot be true that p ≥ 12 and p − q ≥ 3 at the same time. The same rule applies to B.

Every score where nobody has won can be reached from 0–0 without passing through a won score. So these conditions describe the reachable scores exactly.

```python
def pre(p, q):
    return And(p >= 0, q >= 0,
               Not(And(p >= 12, p - q >= 3)),
               Not(And(q >= 12, q - p >= 3)))

def post(p, q, r):
    a_won = And(p >= 11, p - q >= 2)
    b_won = And(q >= 11, q - p >= 2)
    return r == If(a_won, 1, If(b_won, 2, 0))
```
